       for (int j = 0; j < n-i-1; j++) { 
           if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
              swap(arr[j], arr[j+1]);
           }
       }
   }
}
```

2. Selection Sort:
Selection sort is an algorithm that selects the smallest element from an unsorted list in each iteration and places that element at the beginning of the unsorted list.

Time Complexity:
- Average case: O(n^2)
- Worst case: O(n^2)
- Best case: O(n^2)

Space Complexity: 
- Worst case: O(1)

C++ code:
```cpp
void selectionSort(int arr[], int n) {
   for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
       int minIndex = i;
       for(int j = i+1; j < n; j++)
           if(arr[j] < arr[minIndex])
               minIndex = j;
       swap(arr[minIndex], arr[i]);
   }
}
```

3. Insertion Sort:
Insertion sort works similarly to the way you sort playing cards in your hands. The array is virtually split into a sorted and an unsorted region. Values from the unsorted region are picked and placed at the correct position in the sorted region.

Time Complexity: 
- Average case: O(n^2)
- Worst case: O(n^2)
- Best case: O(n)

Space Complexity:
- Worst case: O(1)

C++ code:
```cpp
void insertionSort(int arr[], int n) {
   for(int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
       int key = arr[i];
       int j = i - 1;
       while(j >= 0 && arr[j] > key) {
           arr[j + 1] = arr[j];
           j--;
       }
       arr[j + 1] = key;
   }
}
```

4. Merge Sort:
Merge Sort is a type of Divide and Conquer algorithm. It divides input array into two halves, calls itself for the two halves, and then merges the two sorted halves.

Time Complexity: 
- Average case: O(n log(n))
- Worst case: O(n log(n))
- Best case: O(n log(n))

Space Complexity:
- Worst case: O(n)

C++ code:
```cpp
void merge(int arr[], int l, int m, int r) {
    int i = l;
    int j = m + 1;
    vector<int> temp;

    while(i <= m && j<= r) {
        if(arr[i] <= arr[j]) {
            temp.push_back(arr[i]);
            i++;
        } else {
            temp.push_back(arr[j]);
            j++;
        }
    }

    // copy remaining elements of the first half
    while(i <= m) {
        temp.push_back(arr[i]);
        i++;
    }

    // copy remaining elements of the second half
    while(j <= r) {
        temp.push_back(arr[j]);
        j++;
    }

    // copy back to the original array
    for(int i = l; i <= r; i++) {
        arr[i] = temp[i - l];
    }
}

void mergeSort(int arr[], int l, int r) {
    if(l < r) {
        int m = l + (r - l) / 2;        
        mergeSort(arr, l, m);
        mergeSort(arr, m + 1, r);  
        merge(arr, l, m, r);
    }
}
```

5. Quick Sort:
QuickSort is also a type of Divide and Conquer algorithm. It picks an element as pivot and partitions the given array around the picked pivot. There are many different versions of quickSort that pick pivot in different ways.

Time Complexity:  
- Average case: O(n log(n))
- Worst case: O(n^2)
- Best case: O(n log(n))

Space Complexity:
- Worst case: O(log(n))

C++ code:
```cpp
int partition(int arr[], int low, int high) {
    int pivot = arr[high];
    int i = (low - 1);  // Index of smaller element

    for(int j = low; j <= high- 1; j++) {
        if(arr[j] < pivot) {
            i++;
            swap(arr[i], arr[j]);
        }
    }
    swap(arr[i + 1], arr[high]);
    return (i + 1);
}

void quickSort(int arr[], int low, int high) {
    if(low < high) {
        int pi = partition(arr, low, high);

        quickSort(arr, low, pi - 1);
        quickSort(arr, pi + 1, high);
    }
}
```

6. Heap Sort:
Heap Sort is a comparison-based sorting technique based on Binary Heap data structure. It is similar to selection sort where we first find the maximum element and place the maximum at the end.

Time Complexity: 
- Average case: O(n log(n))
- Worst case: O(n log(n))
- Best case: O(n log(n))

Space Complexity:
- Worst case: O(1)

C++ code:
```cpp
void heapify(int arr[], int n, int i) {
    int largest = i;
    int left = 2 * i + 1;
    int right = 2 * i + 2;

    if(left < n && arr[left] > arr[largest])
        largest = left;

    if(right < n && arr[right] > arr[largest])
        largest = right;

    if(largest != i) {
        swap(arr[i], arr[largest]);
        heapify(arr, n, largest);
    }
}

void heapSort(int arr[], int n) {
    for(int i = n / 2 - 1; i >= 0; i--)
        heapify(arr, n, i);

    for(int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        swap(arr[0], arr[i]);
        heapify(arr, i, 0);
    }
}
```

In these examples, "swap" is a utility function to swap two elements in the array. It can be implemented as follows:
```cpp
void swap(int& a, int& b) {
    int t = a;
    a = b;
    b = t;
}
```

Please note that these codes do not contain any input-output handling. You need to add suitable I/O handling code to use these functions.

