What is wrong with this C++ code that is supposed to swap two numbers? "#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void swap(int a, int b) {
    int temp = a;
    a = b;
    b = temp;
}

int main() {
    int x = 10;
    int y = 20;
    swap(x, y);
    cout << 'x = ' << x << ', y = ' << y << endl;
    return 0;
}"

answer:
The issue with this code is that the swap function is using call-by-value instead of call-by-reference. This means that when the swap function is called, it creates local copies of the arguments passed. Thus, swapping the local copy doesn't affect the original values of x and y.

Here is the corrected C++ code that would swap the values using call-by-reference:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void swap(int& a, int& b) {
    int temp = a;
    a = b;
    b = temp;
}

int main() {
    int x = 10;
    int y = 20;
    swap(x, y);
    cout << "x = " << x << ", y = " << y << endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This uses a reference for both the parameters in the swap function: `int& a` and `int& b`, which allows the function to modify the original values of x and y.

target_txt:
The problem with your code is that you are passing integer arguments 'a' and 'b' as call by value to the swap function. Call by value means that a new set of variables 'a' and 'b' gets created which are local to the swap function, and whatever is done here doesn't impact the variables with the same name in the calling function (in this case, the main function). 

To modify the actual values of 'x' and 'y' in the main function, you need to pass them as call by reference to the function swap. This can be done by modifying the swap function as follows:

```c++
void swap(int &a, int &b) {
    int temp = a;
    a = b;
    b = temp;
}
```

The '&' operator in front of 'a' and 'b' in the function definition denotes that they are references. When we use references, we tell the compiler that we don't want to create new variables, and instead, we wish to use the existing ones. The changes made to the reference variables will therefore impact the original values of 'x' and 'y'.

